#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442 :topic/id #uuid "9fb9cfe0-2d6b-5be3-a023-bef57b6f3340" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054520 :question/id #uuid "578a205e-7879-5f8e-b7b5-a91a134ec5b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-369" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Power Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054521 :question/id #uuid "24fd4536-65aa-5a8f-bb84-9134f26bc125" :question/math-academy-id "e-370" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "078c9361-898f-5b9f-889e-c92442f882d9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Constant Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 43 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321}] :topic/title "The Constant Multiple Rule for Indefinite Integrals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/difficulty 1.3069182911607868 :topic/id #uuid "c2580f8e-3ba3-5aa9-b1f1-0d8e49986381" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1109 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842}] :topic/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443 :topic/difficulty 1.2233896690425385 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428224 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1361->30. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "f580d418-6860-5b04-b964-2999aa08b42f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105574 :question/id #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" :question/math-academy-id "e-375" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1361 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754}] :topic/title "Integrating the Reciprocal Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608 :topic/difficulty 1.1964810343639471 :topic/id #uuid "5824a36d-203d-5834-aa63-4aceedf31f46" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :topic/math-academy-id 906 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050995}] :topic/title "Introduction to First-Order Linear ODEs"}]}
